---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const flow = `Jira / GitHub / Linear
        │  POST {panel}/api/ingest?source=<source>
        ▼
Admin panel   ── record webhook.received ──▶ run ledger / ingest log
        │  POST {FLEET_WEBHOOK_URL}?source=<source>&token=<token>
        ▼  headers: x-fleet-event, x-github-event, x-github-delivery
Jenkins Generic Webhook Trigger
        │
        ▼
fleetDispatcher → fleetctl normalize → resolve → run → publish`;

const forward = `POST {FLEET_WEBHOOK_URL}?source=<source>&token=<FLEET_WEBHOOK_TOKEN>
Authorization: Bearer <FLEET_WEBHOOK_TOKEN>
x-fleet-event: <event>
x-github-event: <passthrough, GitHub only>
x-github-delivery: <passthrough, GitHub only>
Content-Type: application/json

<raw webhook body, unchanged>`;

const jira = `POST {panel}/api/ingest?source=jira
Authorization: Bearer <FLEET_INGEST_TOKEN>`;

const github = `Payload URL:  {FLEET_INGEST_PUBLIC_URL}?source=github
Content type: application/json
Secret:       <FLEET_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET>
Events:       issues, issue_comment, pull_request_review_comment`;

const linear = `POST {panel}/api/ingest?source=linear
Authorization: Bearer <FLEET_INGEST_TOKEN>
Events: Issue create / update`;

const direct = `POST {jenkins}/generic-webhook-trigger/invoke?token=<fleet-webhook-token>&source=jira`;

const gwt = `properties([
  disableConcurrentBuilds(),
  pipelineTriggers([[
    $class: 'GenericTrigger',
    genericVariables: [[key: 'payload', value: '$', expressionType: 'JSONPath']],
    genericRequestVariables: [[key: 'source', regexpFilter: '']],
    genericHeaderVariables: [
      [key: 'x-fleet-event', regexpFilter: ''],
      [key: 'x-github-event', regexpFilter: ''],
      [key: 'x-github-delivery', regexpFilter: '']
    ],
    tokenCredentialId: 'fleet-webhook-token',
    causeString: 'Fleet webhook ($source)'
  ]])
])`;

const triggerApi = `curl -X POST {panel}/api/trigger \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $FLEET_INGEST_TOKEN" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"source":"jira","event":"jira:issue_created","payload":{"issue":{"key":"ENG-1"}}}'`;

const triggerDry = `# preview the exact request without sending it
curl -X POST {panel}/api/trigger \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $FLEET_INGEST_TOKEN" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"source":"jira","event":"jira:issue_created","dry_run":true,"payload":{}}'`;

const local = `bash scripts/jenkins/setup.sh && bash scripts/jenkins/start.sh
bash scripts/jenkins/prepare-local.sh
bash admin/start.sh            # FLEET_WEBHOOK_URL defaults to the local Jenkins`;

const debug = `# the panel's ingest log shows every webhook and its normalize/resolve outcome
open {panel}/ingest

# dispatcher reporting is enabled by --ingest on normalize/resolve
python3 -m fleetctl resolve --registry registry.yaml --issue-file issue.json --ingest`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Triggers & ingest"
  description="Configure fleet triggers: the admin POST /api/ingest endpoint (record + forward), authentication, Jira/GitHub/Linear webhooks, and the Jenkins Generic Webhook Trigger."
>
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-primary/80">triggers &amp; ingest</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-base-content sm:text-4xl">
      Triggers &amp; ingest
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg">
      A task starts when a PM-tool webhook reaches the dispatcher. Senders POST to the admin panel's
      ingest endpoint, which records the event and forwards it verbatim to the Jenkins Generic
      Webhook Trigger — or you can point senders straight at Jenkins.
    </p>

    <h2>How a trigger flows</h2>
    <pre><code>{flow}</code></pre>

    <h2>The ingest endpoint</h2>
    <p>
      <code>POST /api/ingest</code> has two roles, selected by whether a <code>source</code> is
      present. This is the newest logic: a webhook that carries a source is <strong>recorded and
      forwarded</strong>; everything else is a ledger sink.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Request</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /api/ingest?source=&lt;source&gt;</code></td>
          <td>
            <strong>Inbound webhook.</strong> Records <code>webhook.received</code> (source, event,
            payload) and forwards the raw body to <code>FLEET_WEBHOOK_URL</code>. The{" "}
            <code>source</code> may also arrive as an <code>x-fleet-source</code> header.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /api/ingest</code> (no source)</td>
          <td>
            <strong>Ledger sink.</strong> Records dispatcher lifecycle and debug events
            (<code>run.started</code>, <code>run.finished</code>, <code>audit.event</code>,
            <code>webhook.received</code>, <code>normalize.result</code>,
            <code>resolve.result</code>). Never forwarded.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>POST /api/trigger</code></td>
          <td>
            Low-level: forward to the dispatcher <em>without</em> the ingest hop (same path the
            Trigger page and scripts use). Restricted to <code>jira</code>, <code>linear</code>,
            <code>github</code>; injects the <code>webhookEvent</code> field.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>GET /api/metrics</code></td>
          <td>Prometheus text-format ledger metrics.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Event name</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Set with <code>?event=</code> or the <code>x-fleet-event</code> header.</li>
      <li>For GitHub the dispatcher derives it from the passed-through <code>X-GitHub-Event</code> header.</li>
      <li>The normalizer decides whether the event is actionable; unknown sources/events are rejected there.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Authentication</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;FLEET_INGEST_TOKEN&gt;</code> — used by the panel's own calls, the dispatcher, scripts, and PM tools that can set headers.</li>
      <li><code>X-Hub-Signature-256</code> HMAC over the raw body — for GitHub, whose webhooks cannot set custom headers. Set the shared secret as <code>FLEET_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET</code> (the same value as the GitHub webhook's <strong>Secret</strong>).</li>
      <li>Comparison is constant-time. When neither the token nor the secret is configured the API is open — <strong>dev only</strong>.</li>
      <li>Invalid or missing credentials return <code>401</code>; invalid payloads return <code>422</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Response</h3>
    <pre><code>{`201 { "ok": true, "id": 42, "forward": { "status": 200 } }
201 { "ok": true, "id": 42, "forward": { "dry_run": true } }
201 { "ok": true, "id": 42, "forward": { "error": "webhook endpoint not configured" } }`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Forwarding to Jenkins</h2>
    <p>
      The panel forwards the payload <strong>unchanged</strong> — no field injection — so the
      dispatcher sees exactly what the PM tool sent. The token is sent both as a query parameter and
      as a bearer header; retries are disabled.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{forward}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The forwarding target is trusted configuration (<code>FLEET_WEBHOOK_URL</code> /{" "}
      <code>FLEET_WEBHOOK_TOKEN</code>) and is never supplied by the caller. The public URL of the
      panel's own ingest endpoint is <code>FLEET_INGEST_PUBLIC_URL</code> (falls back to the panel's
      endpoint URL).
    </p>

    <h2>Configure a source</h2>

    <h3>GitHub (recommended path)</h3>
    <p>GitHub authenticates with its webhook Secret; the panel records and forwards it.</p>
    <pre><code>{github}</code></pre>

    <h3>Jira</h3>
    <p>
      If your Jira installation can set an <code>Authorization</code> header on webhooks, point it at
      the panel. Otherwise, Jira Cloud cannot set custom headers — send Jira webhooks{" "}
      <strong>directly to Jenkins</strong>, whose Generic Webhook Trigger authenticates with the
      token in the query string.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{jira}

# or directly to Jenkins (no panel ingest-log entry):
{direct}</code></pre>

    <h3>Linear</h3>
    <p>
      Same trade-off as Jira: use the panel with a bearer header when supported, or send Linear
      events straight to the Jenkins trigger URL with <code>&amp;source=linear</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{linear}</code></pre>

    <h2>Jenkins Generic Webhook Trigger</h2>
    <p>
      The trigger is declared by the pipeline's <code>properties(...)</code> block, so it registers on
      the first build. It exposes the raw body as <code>payload</code> and the request/header values
      the dispatcher expects.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{gwt}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Create a Jenkins string credential named <code>fleet-webhook-token</code>; its value is the{" "}
      <code>token</code> in the invoke URL and in <code>FLEET_WEBHOOK_TOKEN</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Panel trigger page &amp; API</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>/trigger</code> page and <code>POST /api/trigger</code> let you fire a webhook-like
      event through the dispatcher without a real PM tool — useful for end-to-end tests. Use{" "}
      <code>dry_run</code> to preview the exact request without sending.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{triggerApi}

{triggerDry}</code></pre>

    <h2>Local development</h2>
    <p>
      The bundled local Jenkins and panel wire this up end-to-end with placeholder tokens. See the
      security model before exposing them.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{local}</code></pre>

    <h2>Debugging</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>/ingest</code> page shows every incoming webhook, the normalization outcome
      (<code>ok</code> / <code>filtered</code> / <code>error</code>), and the routing decision — the
      fastest way to see why an event did not start a run.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{debug}</code></pre>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
      Read the <a href="https://github.com/wrbl606/fleet/blob/main/docs/security.md">security model</a>{" "}
      or the <a href="https://github.com/wrbl606/fleet/blob/main/docs/fleet-contract.md">.fleet/ contract</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
